##[ Hardware Script Standard Library - Mathematical Constants
    
    This file defines mathematical and physical constants for the Hardware Script compiler.
    These constants are available globally without import and are resolved at parse time.
    
    The parser replaces constant names with their values during AST construction,
    enabling compile-time constant folding and eliminating runtime lookups.
]##

## ============================================================================
## MATHEMATICAL CONSTANTS
## ============================================================================

const PI: 3.14159265358979323846
const E: 2.71828182845904523536

## ============================================================================
## PHYSICAL CONSTANTS
## ============================================================================

const SPEED_OF_LIGHT: 299792458  # m/s
const VACUUM_PERMITTIVITY: 8.854187817e-12  # F/m (ε₀)
const VACUUM_PERMEABILITY: 1.25663706212e-6  # H/m (µ₀)
const BOLTZMANN_CONSTANT: 1.380649e-23  # J/K

## ============================================================================
## NOTES FOR USERS
## ============================================================================
##
## HOW TO ADD A NEW CONSTANT:
## 1. Use the format: const NAME: value
## 2. Use UPPERCASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES for constant names
## 3. Add a comment with units (if applicable)
## 4. Save this file - no compiler recompilation needed!
##
## WHAT THE COMPILER DOES WITH THIS:
## - Parses constants at startup into the Prelude layer
## - Replaces constant names with values during parsing
## - Enables compile-time constant folding (e.g., PI * 2 becomes 6.28...)
## - Constants are available globally without import
##
## EXAMPLES OF USE:
## - impedance: 50 * PI  # Calculates at compile time
## - wavelength: SPEED_OF_LIGHT / frequency
## - thermal_noise: BOLTZMANN_CONSTANT * temperature
##
## WHY THESE CONSTANTS?
## - PI, E: Universal mathematical constants
## - SPEED_OF_LIGHT: Needed for high-speed signal timing calculations
## - VACUUM_PERMITTIVITY: Essential for calculating capacitance
## - VACUUM_PERMEABILITY: Essential for calculating inductance
## - BOLTZMANN_CONSTANT: Needed for thermal noise calculations
##
